    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and the build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `sharesHousehold` / `householdId` feature. The controller already had the `sharesHousehold` conflict-bypass logic in place; what was missing was assigning and returning a stable `householdId`.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a read-only `householdId` field to the `Owner` schema (so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it back on `GET /api/owners/{id}`).\n\n2. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 Added a `householdId` field mapped to a `household_id` column, with getter/setter (MapStruct maps it to/from the DTO automatically by name).\n\n3. **DB schemas** (`h2`, `hsqldb`, `mysql`, `postgres`) \u2014 Added the `household_id` column to the `owners` table. Tests run on hsqldb.\n\n4. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 When `sharesHousehold` is true, it now computes and sets `householdId` via a new `householdId(lastName, address)` helper. The id is derived deterministically as the first 12 upper-case hex chars of `SHA-256(\"<normalizedLastName>|<normalizedAddress>\")`, reusing the existing `normalize` logic (case-insensitive, whitespace-collapsed). Because it's a pure function of the household's lastName+address, every owner joining the same household is assigned the identical, stable identifier regardless of creation order \u2014 satisfying \"assign both the same householdId.\"\n\nThe acceptance test (`coreJoinersShareHouseholdId`) and the full suite pass.",
